The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (gdev->dev.release) {
    /* release() has been registered by gpiochip_setup_dev() */
    gpio_device_put(gdev);
    goto err_print_message;
}
```
```c
err_free_label:
    kfree_const(gdev->label);
err_free_descs:
    kfree(gdev->descs);
```
Reason: After commit aab5c6f20023, `gdev->dev.release` is never set (since `device_initialize()` is called but the release function is only assigned during successful registration in `gpiochip_setup_dev()`). The error path checks `gdev->dev.release` to decide whether to call `gpio_device_put()`, but because it's always NULL, the reference count to `gdev->dev` is never dropped, causing resource leaks. Additionally, the manual cleanups (`kfree_const`, `kfree`, etc.) can lead to double-free errors if `gpiodev_release()` eventually runs and also frees the same memory.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
device_initialize(&gdev->dev);
```
```c
err_put_device:
    gpio_device_put(gdev);
    goto err_print_message;
```
Reason: `device_initialize()` is called early, before any operations that might fail. After this point, `gpiodev_release()` (registered via `gcdev_register()`) takes ownership of freeing all resources associated with `gdev->dev`. The new error label `err_put_device` unconditionally drops the device reference via `gpio_device_put()`, which ensures proper cleanup even if registration fails. The previous conditional check and manual kfree blocks are removed, preventing both leaks and double-frees.

